> >> So, you are exporting all chip-effect estimates (thetaA, thetaB)
> >> across all samples and per chromosome.  First, you are aware that
> >> (thetaA,thetaB) are only defined for SNPs, whereas for non-polymorphic
> >> signals you will only have a value corresponding to the thetaA
> >> position whereas thetaB is NA, correct?  BTW, this is also the reason
> >> why we don't label the theta dimension as "A" and "B", because it mean
> >> more than that.
> >>
> >> Second, yes, they are on the intensity scale (non-logged) and they are
> >> not ratios.
> >>
> >> Third, if you downstream are calculating total signals, theta = thetaA
> >> + thetaB (for SNPs), and allele B fractions (BAFs), beta =
> >> thetaB/theta, there is a much easier way.  Simply use:
> >>
> >> data <- extractTotalAndFreqB(ces, units=units);
> >>
> >> above instead of extractTheta(ces, units=units);
> >>
> >>
> >>
> >>
> >> Moreover, you can export the the Affymetrix-specific chip-effect
> >> estimates to technology-independent (total,fracB) data sets by:
> >>
> >> dsList <- exportTotalAndFracB(cesN);
> >>
> >> and you'll get a data set containing the total signals (which are
> >> defined for SNPs and non-polymorphic loci) and a data set containing
> >> the BAFs (defined only for SNPs; non-polymorphic loci has NAs).  We're
> >> moving toward using this standard everywhere.  See how-to page 'Export
> >> total copy numbers and allele B fractions as aroma binary data files':
> >>
> >>    http://aroma-project.org/howtos/exportTotalAndFracB
> >>
> >> Given these, you can then easily extract total and BAF signals (e.g.
> >> http://aroma-project.org/vignettes/tumorboost-highlevel) or write them
> >> to tab-delimited files
> >> (http://aroma-project.org/howtos/writeDataFrame).
> >>
> >>
> >>
> >> Finally, you should also be aware that we are introducing so called
> >> block functions ("blocks"), cf. http://aroma-project.org/blocks/.
> >> This allows you to replace all of the above with:
> >>
> >> library("aroma.affymetrix");
> >> cdf <- AffymetrixCdfFile$byChipType("GenomeWideSNP_6", tags="Full");
> >> csR <- AffymetrixCelSet$byName("Project1",  cdf=cdf);
> >> dsList <- doASCRMAv2(csR, verbose=verbose);
> >>
> >> which gets you the point after exportTotalAndFracB() above, cf.
> >> http://aroma-project.org/vignettes/tumorboost-highlevel
> >>
> >>
> >> Hope this helps
> >>
> >> /Henrik
> >>
> >>>
> >>>> and then I normalized them against my pool of
> >>>> normals or against them matched normal if available  just subtracting the
> >>>> values.
> >>>
> >>> I need to see your code to know what you are doing.
> >>>
> >>>> For ACNE it seems I have to specify the reference, so I created folders
> >>>> for
> >>>> each of my matched pairs and used
> >>>>
> >>>>> NmfSnpPlm(csN, mergeStrands=TRUE, refs=c(FALSE, TRUE))
> >>>>
> >>>> To indicate that the second array is the normal and the first the tumor.
> >>>>
> >>>> Is this the best way to proceed in my situation?
> >>>
> >>> Nope.  When you find yourself having to do tedious "tricks" such as
> >>> splitting up your data set to data sets containing a single
> >>> tumor-normal pair take it as sign for "there must be a better way to
> >>> do this" (which I guess is one reason why you posted this message).
> >>>
> >>> That’s what I was thinking! :-)
> >>>
> >>>>
> >>>> The former command produces the following values for chromosome 2:
> >>>>
> >>>>> summary(data)
> >>>> total             freqB
> >>>> Min.   :    0.0   Min.   :    NA
> >>>>  1st Qu.:    0.0   1st Qu.:    NA
> >>>>  Median :  905.6   Median :    NA
> >>>>  Mean   : 2345.2   Mean   :   NaN
> >>>>  3rd Qu.: 4192.8   3rd Qu.:    NA
> >>>>  Max.   :53200.6   Max.   :    NA
> >>>>                   NA's   :153732
> >>>>
> >>>> So it's clear that I'm doing something wrong. Does anyone know what is it?
> >>>
> >>> The problem is that you are asking the ACNE method to estimate the
> >>> (SNP-specific) crosstalk parameters using only two arrays, actually
> >>> only one since you specify that it is only the normal sample that
> >>> should be used for the estimation.  The model parameters are
> >>> non-identifiable with only one sample, i.e. it is not possible to
> >>> estimate the ACNE parameter.
> >>>
> >>> It should probably be added to the ACNE method/estimator (somewhere in
> >>> the NmfSnpPlm class) to assert that not too few reference samples are
> >>> used, instead of returning missing values.
> >>>
> >>> Instead, you should keep all your samples in the same directory and
> >>> process the whole data set at once.  You can specify which samples
> >>> should be used as the reference set by using argument 'refs' to
> >>> NmfSnpPlm, just as above.  For example:
> >>>
> >>> refs <- seq(from=2, to=length(csN), by=2);
> >>> nmf <- NmfSnpPlm(csN, mergeStrands=TRUE, refs=refs);
> >>>
> >>> Note that you might use a more clever approach to identify which
> >>> samples are normals.  For example, if the normal samples contain the
> >>> tag "N", you can do:
> >>>
> >>> refs <- which(sapply(csN, hasTag, "N"));
> >>>
> >>> Hope this solves your problem.
> >>>
> >>> /Henrik
